      "text": "To determine the area of the figure \\(ABCD\\), we first need to understand the structure of the figure. The figure \\(ABCD\\) consists of two sectors of a circle of radius 12, each with a central angle of \\(60^\\circ\\). The two sectors overlap in the middle, forming a rhombus.\n\n1. **Calculate the area of one sector:**\n   The area of a full circle with radius 12 is:\n   \\[\n   \\text{Area of the full circle} = \\pi \\times 12^2 = 144\\pi\n   \\]\n   Since each sector has a central angle of \\(60^\\circ\\), which is \\(\\frac{1}{6}\\) of a full circle, the area of one sector is:\n   \\[\n   \\text{Area of one sector} = \\frac{1}{6} \\times 144\\pi = 24\\pi\n   \\]\n\n2. **Calculate the area of the rhombus:**\n   The rhombus formed by the two sectors has side length equal to the radius of the circle, which is 12. To find the area of the rhombus, we note that it can be divided into two congruent 30-60-90 right triangles. The height of each triangle (which is the side length of the rhombus) is 12, and the base is:\n   \\[\n   \\text{Base} = 12 \\cos 60^\\circ = 12 \\times \\frac{1}{2} = 6\n   \\]\n   The area of one 30-60-90 triangle is:\n   \\[\n   \\text{Area of one triangle} = \\frac{1}{2} \\times 6 \\times 12 \\times \\sin 60^\\circ = \\frac{1}{2} \\times 6 \\times 12 \\times \\frac{\\sqrt{3}}{2} = 18\\sqrt{3}\n   \\]\n   Therefore, the area of the rhombus (which is twice the area of one triangle) is:\n   \\[\n   \\text{Area of the rhombus} = 2 \\times 18\\sqrt{3} = 36\\sqrt{3}\n   \\]\n\n3. **Calculate the area of figure \\(ABCD\\):**\n   The area of figure \\(ABCD\\) is the sum of the area of the two sectors and the area of the rhombus:\n   \\[\n   \\text{Area of figure } ABCD = 2 \\times 24\\pi + 36\\sqrt{3} = 48\\pi + 36\\sqrt{3}\n   \\]\n   Therefore, the area of figure \\(ABCD\\) is:\n   \\[\n   \\boxed{48\\pi + 36\\sqrt{3}}\n   \\]",
