    {
      "sample_index": 3,
      "text": "To determine the area of the figure \\(ABCD\\), we need to analyze the given sectors and their placement. The figure consists of two sectors, each with a central angle of \\(60^\\circ\\), and the line segments connecting the endpoints of these sectors are radii of the circle and form a straight line. This configuration creates a regular hexagon with each internal angle being \\(60^\\circ\\), hence it is a regular hexagon.\n\n### Step-by-Step Solution:\n\n1. **Determine the length of each side of the hexagon:**\n   Since the sectors are placed side by side and they form a straight line, the length of each side of the hexagon is equal to the radius of the circle. Given that the radius of the circle is \\(12\\), each side of the hexagon is \\(12\\).\n\n2. **Calculate the area of the hexagon:**\n   A regular hexagon can be divided into 6 equilateral triangles, each with a side length equal to the radius of the circle. The area of one equilateral triangle with side length \\(s\\) is given by:\n   \\[\n   \\text{Area of one triangle} = \\frac{\\sqrt{3}}{4} s^2\n   \\]\n   Here, \\(s = 12\\). Therefore, the area of one triangle is:\n   \\[\n   \\text{Area of one triangle} = \\frac{\\sqrt{3}}{4} \\times 12^2 = \\frac{\\sqrt{3}}{4} \\times 144 = 36\\sqrt{3}\n   \\]\n   Since the hexagon consists of 6 such triangles, the area of the hexagon is:\n   \\[\n   \\text{Area of the hexagon} = 6 \\times 36\\sqrt{3} = 216\\sqrt{3}\n   \\]\n\nTherefore, the area of the figure \\(ABCD\\) is \\(\\boxed{216\\sqrt{3}}\\).",
